Tools the config references but no installer installs, or only one does

Several tools are either never installed or installed on exactly one platform, so the shell
config references things a freshly provisioned machine does not have:

- eza is used by zsh/alias.zsh:26 and no installer installs it anywhere.
- htop is Arch-only (install-archlinux.sh:80). lazygit and lazydocker are Arch-only too
  (:380). vim is commented out at install-ubuntu.sh:258, and Arch installs vi, not vim.
- terraform and packer are in the base install-ubuntu.sh only, so Arch and Amazon Linux
  never get them. They are infrastructure tools rather than base ones, so a separate
  make/install-infra.sh fits the existing layout better than three copies of the logic.
- fcitx5 is installed ungated at install-ubuntu.sh:134, unlike Arch where it now sits
  behind DOTFILES_HEADLESS. An input method framework is not useful on a headless box.

Candidate new optional sets, alongside the existing six:

- install-cli.sh        eza, bat, fd, delta, zoxide, dust, duf, btop
- install-security.sh   sops, age, gitleaks, trivy. The repo has detect-private-key and
                        detect-aws-credentials pre-commit hooks but no tool for actually
                        storing secrets, which is the half that matters.
- kubernetes additions  stern, and argocd or flux. kubectx/kubens are already covered by
                        the krew ctx/ns plugins, so they are not needed separately.
- aws additions         granted or aws-vault.
- llm additions         aider.

One caveat for any of this: test-install.sh asserts none of the optional tools, so
`make aws && make test` is green whether or not the AWS CLI actually installed. A new set
should arrive with assertions, scoped per platform the way the existing ones now are.
